<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kosa Pan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kosa Pan was a Siamese diplomat who led King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a>'s embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1686. The great curiosity aroused by the Siamese envoys made the embassy famous in France. After Narai's death he supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phetracha">Phetracha</a> and served as minister of trade and foreign affairs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-khlang">Phra Khlang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kosa-pan.jpg" alt="Kosa Pan" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Benoist (painter) (1632–1717) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kosa_Pan_of_Siam_Portrait_by_Antoine_Benoist_1686.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
